-fourier-transform/</link><pubDate>Thu, 07 Nov 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/04-the-quantum-fourier-transform/</guid><description>The last three posts built circuits whose payoff was a single global fact read out by interference.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/04-the-quantum-fourier-transform/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Phase estimation</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/05-phase-estimation/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/05-phase-estimation/</guid><description>The last post built the Quantum Fourier Transform and promised it was a readout instrument.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/05-phase-estimation/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Shor&amp;#39;s algorithm</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/06-shors-algorithm/</link><pubDate>Fri, 04 Apr 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/06-shors-algorithm/</guid><description>This is the one everyone has heard of: the algorithm that factors integers in polynomial time and, if a big enough quantum computer is ever built, breaks RSA.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/06-shors-algorithm/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Scaling the simulator</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/07-scaling-the-simulator/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Jun 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/07-scaling-the-simulator/</guid><description>This post does not add a quantum idea.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/07-scaling-the-simulator/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Mixed states and the density matrix</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/08-mixed-states-and-the-density-matrix/</link><pubDate>Sat, 30 Aug 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/08-mixed-states-and-the-density-matrix/</guid><description>Every state in this series so far has been a single vector with definite amplitudes.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/08-mixed-states-and-the-density-matrix/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Noise and decoherence</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/09-noise-and-decoherence/</link><pubDate>Thu, 13 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/09-noise-and-decoherence/</guid><description>Last post ended with a warning: the off-diagonal coherences of a density matrix are the fragile part, and a real qubit loses them on its own.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/09-noise-and-decoherence/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Quantum error correction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/10-quantum-error-correction/</link><pubDate>Mon, 26 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/10-quantum-error-correction/</guid><description>Last post was the bad news: a real qubit leaks its coherence into the environment and forgets what it was doing.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/10-quantum-error-correction/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>The engine, in full</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/11-the-engine-in-full/</link><pubDate>Fri, 10 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/11-the-engine-in-full/</guid><description>Across the series I kept saying the simulator was small: a qubit is an array, a gate is a matrix, the whole thing is a few hundred lines.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/11-the-engine-in-full/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Epilogue: measure and meaning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/12-epilogue-measure-and-meaning/</link><pubDate>Tue, 23 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/12-epilogue-measure-and-meaning/</guid><description>The very first useful thing our simulator did, back in post 0, was turn a vector of amplitudes into a vector of probabilities.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/12-epilogue-measure-and-meaning/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>Classical Information First: The Coin Under the Cup</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/00-classical-information/</link><pubDate>Mon, 08 Jan 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/00-classical-information/</guid><description>Before the qubit, the ordinary object it edits: classical probability as vectors and stochastic matrices, and the one rule (weights never go negative) that quantum information breaks.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/00-classical-information/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Quantum Computing from Scratch</media:title></media:content><category>quantum-computing</category><category>information</category><category>python</category><category>from-scratch</category></item></channel></rss>